.TH WISP 8 "2026-09-15" "wisp 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
wisp \- build a reproducible initramfs from declared inputs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wisp build
.RI [ \-k " version" ]
.RI [ \-o " file" ]
.RB [ \-\-compress
.IR none | zstd ]
.RI [ \-\-manifest " file" ]
.RI [ \-\-stage " dir" ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.br
.B wisp manifest
.RI [ \-k " version" ]
.RI [ \-\-stage " dir" ]
.br
.B wisp resolve
.RI [ \-k " version" ]
.IR selector ...
.br
.B update-initramfs
.BR \-c | \-u | \-d
.RI [ \-k " version" | all ]
.RI [ \-b " dir" ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wisp
assembles an initramfs whose bytes are a pure function of a manifest and the
files it names: the kernel package's module tree, the firmware and microcode
files of their packages, the init and the keymap from the wisp package, and
the small configuration files the builder generates. It does not scan the
running machine for loaded modules or hardware, does not copy binaries found
by their shared libraries, and does not stamp file system times into the
archive. Two machines with the same package versions and the same declared
inputs produce the same bytes.
.PP
The initramfs holds one static program as
.IR /init ,
.BR wisp-init (7),
the resolved kernel modules with the firmware files they name, the measured
configuration
.BR wisp.conf (5)
and, when configured, a keymap, a volume table, a detached header copy and a
key provider; the CPU microcode is prepended as an early cpio.
.PP
The same binary is installed as
.B /usr/sbin/update-initramfs
and as the kernel maintainer hooks under
.IR /etc/kernel/postinst.d " and " /etc/kernel/postrm.d ,
so kernel packages and other software keep working as with initramfs-tools.
The package also listens to the dpkg trigger
.B update-initramfs
that linux-firmware, kmod, udev, cryptsetup, lvm2 and others activate, and to
file changes under the microcode directories of /lib/firmware; each rebuilds
the newest kernel's initramfs as
.B update-initramfs -u
does.
.SH VERBS
.TP
.B build
Assemble the manifest for the kernel (see
.BR wisp.conf (5)
for the inputs), resolve the modules, and write the archive to
.IR /boot/initrd.img\- version
(or
.BR \-o ).
The output is written to a temporary file and renamed, so a failed build
leaves the previous initrd untouched. With
.B \-\-manifest
an explicit manifest is built instead of assembling one; its first line is
.BR "format = 1" ,
and a manifest of another format, or one with a key or section this version
does not know, is refused rather than built differently. With
.B \-\-stage
the assembled manifest and every generated file are kept in
.IR dir ,
which is what a verifier needs to rebuild the same bytes elsewhere.
.B \-v
lists the modules that went in. The compressed size, the SHA-256 of the
output and the SHA-256 of the uncompressed archive are printed.
.TP
.B manifest
Print the manifest that
.B build
would use.
.TP
.B resolve
Show the module resolution for names and selectors against
.IR /lib/modules/ version :
the files in load order, the names that are built into the kernel, and any
that are missing.
.TP
.B update-initramfs
The initramfs-tools compatible facade.
.B \-c
and
.B \-u
build,
.B \-d
deletes;
.B \-k
selects a version or
.BR all ,
the default is the newest installed kernel;
.B \-b
selects the boot directory.
.B \-t
is accepted and ignored. Unknown options are an error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/wisp/wisp.conf
The runtime configuration, completed by the builder and placed in the initramfs.
.TP
.I /etc/wisp/modules
Module names and directory selectors; a built-in list is used when absent.
.TP
.I /etc/wisp/lvm.tab
A hand-maintained volume table; otherwise recorded from the running system.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/wisp/key/
Key providers, see
.BR wisp.conf (5).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on any failure. The kernel hooks return 2 when called without a version.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wisp.conf (5),
.BR wisp-init (7)
